[[:db/cas 17592186481410 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}-xy=x(x-y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(3,2) & =(3,3-2) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,4) & =(5,5-4) \\\\  & =(5,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,2) & =(5,5-2) \\\\  & =(5,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,0) & =(3,3-0) \\\\  & =(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 5,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/e0/e0b810c03e7e3210fcff3a739e3781ad5ff694bfe44d5f73d7782c3b4108a286.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}x(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{3}^{5}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{3}^{5}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}-xy=x(x-y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(3,2) & =(3,3-2) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,4) & =(5,5-4) \\\\  & =(5,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,2) & =(5,5-2) \\\\  & =(5,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,0) & =(3,3-0) \\\\  & =(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 5,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/e0/e0b810c03e7e3210fcff3a739e3781ad5ff694bfe44d5f73d7782c3b4108a286.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}x(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{3}^{5}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{3}^{5}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481414 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{x})=-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{1}{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(1,2).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,2)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{y}{x} & \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{x})=-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{1}{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(1,2).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,2)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481418 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y{cos}^{2}⁡(y-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}v{cos}^{2}⁡u\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v{cos}^{2}⁡u\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2u+1)du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2u}{2}+u]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\pi v\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y{cos}^{2}⁡(y-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}v{cos}^{2}⁡u\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v{cos}^{2}⁡u\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2u+1)du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2u}{2}+u]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\pi v\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481422 :question/problem "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $3{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $3{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481422 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-8+2}{1+4+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n3{x}_{2}=3\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3})=-2.\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-8+2}{1+4+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n3{x}_{2}=3\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3})=-2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481423 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x,\\,v=x+2y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-\\frac{1}{2}) & =(2\\cdot 1,1+2(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =(2,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3}{4}) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}+2(-\\frac{3}{4})) \\\\  & =(3,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\pi -3}{4}) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}+2(\\frac{\\pi -3}{4})) \\\\  & =(3,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}) & =(2\\cdot 1,1+2(\\frac{\\pi -2}{4})) \\\\  & =(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/dd/dd628fd99b4684d79e8c3b0f4e6696f72582abaef18eef729f5611711a2e42ec.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,|\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{3}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{2}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡v]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x,\\,v=x+2y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-\\frac{1}{2}) & =(2\\cdot 1,1+2(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =(2,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3}{4}) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}+2(-\\frac{3}{4})) \\\\  & =(3,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\pi -3}{4}) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}+2(\\frac{\\pi -3}{4})) \\\\  & =(3,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}) & =(2\\cdot 1,1+2(\\frac{\\pi -2}{4})) \\\\  & =(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/dd/dd628fd99b4684d79e8c3b0f4e6696f72582abaef18eef729f5611711a2e42ec.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,|\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{3}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{2}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡v]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481424 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=u-2v,\\,y=u-v.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{(u-2v)}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=u-2v,\\,y=u-v.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{(u-2v)}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481429 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+1,\\,v=y+(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows::\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2(x+1) & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=x+1,\\,v=y+(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=u-1,\\,y=v-{u}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y+1)\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}[(u-1)-(v-{u}^{2})+1]|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\cdot |1|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+1,\\,v=y+(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows::\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2(x+1) & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=x+1,\\,v=y+(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=u-1,\\,y=v-{u}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y+1)\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}[(u-1)-(v-{u}^{2})+1]|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\cdot |1|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481430 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2}=(2x+y)(x-y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-1) & =(2(1)+(-1),1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,0) & =(2(2)+0,2-0) \\\\  & =(4,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,-2) & =(2(3)+(-2),3-(-2)) \\\\  & =(4,5) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-3) & =(2(2)+(-3),2-(-3)) \\\\  & =(1,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,2\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/9f/9f5030bc3437fbc4431dd5350620bc7f9133d2bf910654607c0c5f7ef292f3a3.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{1}^{4}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{2}^{5}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{21}{2}\\cdot \\frac{15}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{105}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2}=(2x+y)(x-y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-1) & =(2(1)+(-1),1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,0) & =(2(2)+0,2-0) \\\\  & =(4,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,-2) & =(2(3)+(-2),3-(-2)) \\\\  & =(4,5) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-3) & =(2(2)+(-3),2-(-3)) \\\\  & =(1,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,2\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/9f/9f5030bc3437fbc4431dd5350620bc7f9133d2bf910654607c0c5f7ef292f3a3.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{1}^{4}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{2}^{5}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{21}{2}\\cdot \\frac{15}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{105}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481431 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }-1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)(x-y)^{3/2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{3/2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}u{v}^{3/2}|-\\frac{1}{2}|du\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}[{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}[\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}]dv \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}\\,dv \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{2}{5}\\,{v}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}[1-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }-1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)(x-y)^{3/2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{3/2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}u{v}^{3/2}|-\\frac{1}{2}|du\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}[{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}[\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}]dv \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}\\,dv \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{2}{5}\\,{v}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}[1-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481432 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}){e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)^{2}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{u}^{2}{e}^{v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{u}^{2}{e}^{v}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}[{e}^{v}]_{0}^{2}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}\\cdot ({e}^{2}-1)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13({e}^{2}-1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}){e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)^{2}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{u}^{2}{e}^{v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{u}^{2}{e}^{v}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}[{e}^{v}]_{0}^{2}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}\\cdot ({e}^{2}-1)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13({e}^{2}-1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481433 :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$"] [:db/cas 17592186481433 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)+7\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{46} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{23} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{46}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{46} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{23} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{46}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{46},$ $b=\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{23},$ $c=-\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{46}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{46}|+|\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{23}|+|-\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{46}|=\\frac{5\\sqrt{46}}{23}.\n$$" "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)+7\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{46} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{23} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{46}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{46} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{23} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{46}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{46},$ $b=\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{23},$ $c=-\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{46}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{46}|+|\\frac{3\\sqrt{46}}{23}|+|-\\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{46}|=\\frac{5\\sqrt{46}}{23}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481434 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+y\\le \\pi ,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi \\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]_{0}^{\\pi }\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot 2\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,du \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡u]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+y\\le \\pi ,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi \\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]_{0}^{\\pi }\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot 2\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,du \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡u]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481440 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }-1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}(x+y)(x-y)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{4}v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})\\,du \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,du \\\\  & =3[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }-1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}(x+y)(x-y)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{4}v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})\\,du \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,du \\\\  & =3[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481579 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481589 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481593 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481599 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481602 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481604 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481607 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481608 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481613 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481614 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481620 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481624 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481625 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481626 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481630 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481636 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481637 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481647 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481648 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481649 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186481667 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal.   Round the answer to $2$ decimal places." "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal.   Round the answer to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186481667 :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,3\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{11}\\sqrt{27}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{11})≈{58.52}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,3\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{11}\\sqrt{27}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{11})≈{58.52}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."] [:db/cas 17592186481669 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal." "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal."] [:db/cas 17592186481669 :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\},$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=0[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=0[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{(0)(0)+(-1)(-1)+(0)(2)+(1)(-1)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=0[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Since we got the zero-vector as the projection, the vector $x$ must be  orthogonal to the subspace $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ Thus, the vector $x$ extends the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}.$" "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\},$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=0\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=0\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{(0)(0)+(-1)(-1)+(0)(2)+(1)(-1)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=0\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Since we got the zero-vector as the projection, the vector $x$ must be  orthogonal to the subspace $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ Thus, the vector $x$ extends the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186481670 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal."] [:db/cas 17592186481670 :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{12\\cdot 5+(-1)\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-1)}{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{12\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-5)+(-2)\\cdot 0}{{2}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The projection is the vector $x$ itself. This means that $x$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ In other words, $x$ lies in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$" "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{12\\cdot 5+(-1)\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-1)}{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{12\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-5)+(-2)\\cdot 0}{{2}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The projection is the vector $x$ itself. This means that $x$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ In other words, $x$ lies in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186481672 :question/problem "Consider the ve