ix}-1 & 9 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186483345 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ above. If the normal equation corresponding to the system $Ax=b$ is given below, what is the value of $a-b-c?$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}18 & 1 & 36 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ b & 2 & a\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ above. If the normal equation corresponding to the system $Ax=b$ is given below, what is the value of $a-b-c?$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}18 & 1 & 36 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ b & 2 & a\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483345 :question/worked-solution "The normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}18 & 1 & 36 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 36 & 2 & 72\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na=72,\\,b=36,\\,c=20,\n$$\n\nand hence\n\n$$\na-b-c=72-36-20=16.\n$$" "The normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}18 & 1 & 36 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 36 & 2 & 72\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na=72,\\,b=36,\\,c=20,\n$$\n\nand hence\n\n$$\na-b-c=72-36-20=16.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483346 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -18 \\\\ 15 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{B}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -18 \\\\ 15 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186483346 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -18 \\\\ 15 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -18 \\\\ 15 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=9 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-18 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -18 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 15\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -18 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 15\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=9 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ -\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=-3.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & =9 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+(-3) & =9 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6+(-3)=3.$" "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -18 \\\\ 15 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -18 \\\\ 15 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=9 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-18 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -18 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 15\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -18 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 15\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=9 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ -\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=-3.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & =9 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+(-3) & =9 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6+(-3)=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186483363 :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=1+t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=1-t+{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=4+2{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=1+t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=1-t+{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=4+2{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186483363 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(1+t)+{x}_{2}(1-t+{t}^{2}) & =4+2{t}^{2} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})t+{x}_{2}{t}^{2} & =4+2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third  equations, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(2)=4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2+2=4.$" "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(1+t)+{x}_{2}(1-t+{t}^{2}) & =4+2{t}^{2} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})t+{x}_{2}{t}^{2} & =4+2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third  equations, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(2)=4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2+2=4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186483371 :question/problem "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $A$ and $B$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(A)=6$, find $det(B).$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $A$ and $B$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(A)=6$, find $det(B).$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483382 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}5t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-3t-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-3t-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186483382 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{3t}-{e}^{3t}\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int t{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=4t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=4\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(4t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =4t{e}^{t}-4{e}^{t} \\\\  & =4{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =t\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{3}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{3}{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}(3t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{t}(t-1) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}(3t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}36(t-1)-(3t+1) \\\\ -(3t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}33t-37 \\\\ -3t-1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{3t}-{e}^{3t}\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}4t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 4t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int t{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=4t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=4\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(4t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =4t{e}^{t}-4{e}^{t} \\\\  & =4{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =t\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{3}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{3}{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}(3t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4{e}^{t}(t-1) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}(3t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}36(t-1)-(3t+1) \\\\ -(3t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}33t-37 \\\\ -3t-1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483389 :question/problem "Consider the polynomials ${p}_{1}(t)$ and ${p}_{2}(t),$ given by\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=1+2t+3{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=-3-t-2{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFind $p(t)$ given that $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\},$ and the coordinates of $p(t)$ relative to $B$ are $[p(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" "Consider the polynomials ${p}_{1}(t)$ and ${p}_{2}(t),$ given by\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=1+2t+3{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=-3-t-2{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFind $p(t)$ given that $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\},$ and the coordinates of $p(t)$ relative to $B$ are $[p(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186483389 :question/worked-solution "Any vector in  $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\}$ can be written a