\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$"] [:db/cas 17592186483398 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\})=2.$" "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186483399 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t} & -{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"60px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"60px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{9}{5}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}4{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}}},\\,Φ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t} & -{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"60px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"60px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{9}{5}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186483399 :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t} & -{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-{e}^{2t})-3{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{-4t}}[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{4t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-2t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{4t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-2t} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{5t} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{5t} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7}{3}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{15}{e}^{5t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t} & -{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{15}{e}^{5t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{9}{5}{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ \\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t} & -{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-{e}^{2t})-3{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{-4t}}\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{4t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-2t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{4t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-2t} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{5t} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{5t} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int \\frac{7}{3}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{15}{e}^{5t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t} & -{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{15}{e}^{5t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{9}{5}{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ \\frac{9}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186483409 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then $2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{B}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ then $2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186483409 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} & 3{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1} & {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=9 \\\\ -{x}_{1}=-2 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=9 \\\\ {x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}=-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second,  third and fourth equations, we get ${x}_{2}=3.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3 & =5 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, $2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2\\cdot 2+3=7.$" "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} & 3{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1} & {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=9 \\\\ -{x}_{1}=-2 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -12\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=9 \\\\ {x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}=-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second,  third and fourth equations, we get ${x}_{2}=3.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3 & =5 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, $2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2\\cdot 2+3=7.$"] [:db/cas 17592186483426 :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=1-t+{t}^{3},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=1+t-{t}^{3},\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2+t+{t}^{2},\\,\\,q(t)=-2t-2{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}=$" "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=1-t+{t}^{3},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=1+t-{t}^{3},\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2+t+{t}^{2},\\,\\,q(t)=-2t-2{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${x}_{1}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186483426 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=q(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(1-t+{t}^{3})+{x}_{2}(1+t-{t}^{3})+{x}_{3}(2+t+{t}^{2}) & =-2t-2{t}^{2} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3})+(-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})t+{x}_{3}{t}^{2}+({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}){t}^{3} & =-2t-2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ {x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-1){R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ {x}_{3}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the  third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2{x}_{2}+3(-2)=-2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the obtained values into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(2)+2(-2)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ and therefore ${x}_{1}=2.$" "We need to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=q(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(1-t+{t}^{3})+{x}_{2}(1+t-{t}^{3})+{x}_{3}(2+t+{t}^{2}) & =-2t-2{t}^{2} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3})+(-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})t+{x}_{3}{t}^{2}+({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}){t}^{3} & =-2t-2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ {x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-1){R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ {x}_{3}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the  third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2{x}_{2}+3(-2)=-2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the obtained values into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(2)+2(-2)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ and therefore ${x}_{1}=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186484193 :question/problem "Find $5det(A)+2det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -1 & 1.5 \\\\ 3 & -9 & 12\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find $5det(A)+2det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -1 & 1.5 \\\\ 3 & -9 & 12\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484193 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has proportional rows, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -1 & 1.5 \\\\ 3 & -9 & 12\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- For the matrix $B,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(B)=2\\cdot 3-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5det(A)+2det(B)=5\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 3=6.$" "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has proportional rows, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -1 & 1.5 \\\\ 3 & -9 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- For the matrix $B,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(B)=2\\cdot 3-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5det(A)+2det(B)=5\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 3=6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186484199 :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(A).$" "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186484199 :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(8-(-3))\\cdot (-5-1) \\\\  & =11\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-66.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(8-(-3))\\cdot (-5-1) \\\\  & =11\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-66.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484212 :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n}(R)$ contains a total of ${n}^{2}$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ contains ${2}^{2}=4$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{9}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{9}(R))={9}^{2}=81.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.\n$$" "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n}(R)$ contains a total of ${n}^{2}$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ contains ${2}^{2}=4$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{9}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{9}(R))={9}^{2}=81.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484213 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has identical columns: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ -7 & 1 & -1 & -7 \\\\ 3 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|A|=0.$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has a zero row: \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0.5 \\\\ 3 & -3 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|B|=0.$\n\n- Since $C$ is a triangular matrix, we obtain \n\n$$\n|C|=7\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-2)=14\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $|A|$ and $|B|$ are equal to $0.$" "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has identical columns: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ -7 & 1 & -1 & -7 \\\\ 3 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|A|=0.$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has a zero row: \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 0.5 \\\\ 3 & -3 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|B|=0.$\n\n- Since $C$ is a triangular matrix, we obtain \n\n$$\n|C|=7\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-2)=14\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $|A|$ and $|B|$ are equal to $0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186484219 :question/problem "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$, and ${q}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${q}_{3}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$, and ${q}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${q}_{3}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484219 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${q}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{3}}={{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{1}}+{{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 2=4.$" "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${q}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{3}}={{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{1}}+{{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 2=4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186484234 :question/problem "Given that $H=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(H).$" "Given that $H=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(H).$"] [:db/cas 17592186484234 :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(H)=det[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $2$nd column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\cdot 3(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(2-(-6)\\cdot 3(2-3) \\\\  & =32\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-96.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(H)=det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $2$nd column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot 3(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(2-(-6)\\cdot 3(2-3) \\\\  & =32\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-96.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484246 :question/problem "The matrix $P$ is given below. Let ${p}_{1}$, ${p}_{2}$,  ${p}_{3}$ and ${p}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $P.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(P).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${p}_{4}={x}_{1}{p}_{1}+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix $P$ is given below. Let ${p}_{1}$, ${p}_{2}$,  ${p}_{3}$ and ${p}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $P.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(P).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${p}_{4}={x}_{1}{p}_{1}+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484246 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $P$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(P)$ is $\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${p}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{4}}={{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{1}}+{{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 2=4.$" "First, we reduce the matrix $P$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(P)$ is $\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${p}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{4}}={{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{1}}+{{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 2=4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186484278 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ is a triangular matrix, we obtain \n\n$$\n|A|=5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 2=-10\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has a zero column: \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|B|=0.$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has identical rows: \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|C|=0.$\n\nTherefore, only $|B|$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$" "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ is a triangular matrix, we obtain \n\n$$\n|A|=5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 2=-10\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has a zero column: \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|B|=0.$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has identical rows: \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|C|=0.$\n\nTherefore, only $|B|$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186484285 :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{5\\times 7}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{5\\times 7}(R))=5\\cdot 7=35.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$" "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{5\\times 7}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{5\\times 7}(R))=5\\cdot 7=35.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484287 :question/problem "The matrix $H$ is given below. Let ${h}_{1}$, ${h}_{2}$, and ${h}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $H.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{h}_{1},{h}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(H).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdo