& 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186485505 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a square matrix\n\nII. ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a square matrix\n\nII. ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix"] [:db/cas 17592186485505 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The number of rows in $A$ is not the same as the number of columns. So, it's not a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\cdot A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\ne I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not a square matrix and ${A}^{T}\\cdot A\\ne I,$ our matrix is **not** orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements.\"" "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The number of rows in $A$ is not the same as the number of columns. So, it's not a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\cdot A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\ne I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not a square matrix and ${A}^{T}\\cdot A\\ne I,$ our matrix is **not** orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements.\""] [:db/cas 17592186485506 :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186485507 :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186485512 :question/problem "Solve for $x$ in $Ax=b$ given that the matrix $A$ is orthogonal, and \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Solve for $x$ in $Ax=b$ given that the matrix $A$ is orthogonal, and \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485512 :question/worked-solution "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nHence, since $A$ is orthogonal, we have ${A}^{-1}={A}^{T}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}Ax & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nHence, since $A$ is orthogonal, we have ${A}^{-1}={A}^{T}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}Ax & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485514 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing $|det(A)|,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|det(PB{P}^{-1})| \\\\  & =|det(PB{P}^{T})| \\\\  & =|det(P)|\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot |det({P}^{T})| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|det(B)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product $(PQ)\\cdot (PQ)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ\\cdot (PQ)^{T} & =(PQ)\\cdot {Q}^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(Q{Q}^{T}){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P{P}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. As a counterexample, consider the following sum involving two orthogonal matrices:\n\n$$\n2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe resulting matrix is not orthogonal since, for example, the first column is not normalized.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing $|det(A)|,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|det(PB{P}^{-1})| \\\\  & =|det(PB{P}^{T})| \\\\  & =|det(P)|\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot |det({P}^{T})| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|det(B)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product $(PQ)\\cdot (PQ)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ\\cdot (PQ)^{T} & =(PQ)\\cdot {Q}^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(Q{Q}^{T}){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P{P}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. As a counterexample, consider the following sum involving two orthogonal matrices:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe resulting matrix is not orthogonal since, for example, the first column is not normalized.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186485516 :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{8} \\\\ \\sqrt{8} & 4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind $|\\text{det}(PQ)|$ given that $P$ is an orthogonal matrix." "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{8} \\\\ \\sqrt{8} & 4\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind $|\\text{det}(PQ)|$ given that $P$ is an orthogonal matrix."] [:db/cas 17592186485518 :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following are orthogonal sets?\n\nI. $U=\\{Q([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{Q([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}])\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{Q([\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}])\\}$" "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following are orthogonal sets?\n\nI. $U=\\{Q(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix})\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{Q(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix})\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{Q(\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix})\\}$"] [:db/cas 17592186485518 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nLet's denote the elements of $U,W,$ and $V$ respectively as \n\n$\\,$ $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\},\\,$ $\\{Q({v}_{1}),Q({v}_{2}),Q({v}_{3})\\},\\,$ and $\\,\\{Q({w}_{1}),Q({w}_{2})\\}.$\n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2})=0,\\,Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0,\\,Q({v}_{2})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0.$\n\n- $Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2}) & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=20-3=17\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not and orthogonal set.\n\n- For the Set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]=3-4+1=0,\\,✓ \\\\ Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=2+8+18=28\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2}) & ={w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]=-15+15=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$W$ only\"." "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nLet's denote the elements of $U,W,$ and $V$ respectively as \n\n$\\,$ $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\},\\,$ $\\{Q({v}_{1}),Q({v}_{2}),Q({v}_{3})\\},\\,$ and $\\,\\{Q({w}_{1}),Q({w}_{2})\\}.$\n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2})=0,\\,Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0,\\,Q({v}_{2})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0.$\n\n- $Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2}) & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=20-3=17\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not and orthogonal set.\n\n- For the Set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}=3-4+1=0,\\,✓ \\\\ Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=2+8+18=28\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2}) & ={w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}=-15+15=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$W$ only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186485524 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a square matrix\n\nII. $A\\cdot {A}^{T}=I$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a square matrix\n\nII. $A\\cdot {A}^{T}=I$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix"] [:db/cas 17592186485524 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{24}{25} \\\\ \\frac{24}{25} & 1\\end{aligned}]\\ne I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A\\cdot {A}^{T}\\ne I,$ our matrix is **not** orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{24}{25} \\\\ \\frac{24}{25} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\ne I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A\\cdot {A}^{T}\\ne I,$ our matrix is **not** orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186485526 :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U.\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ C & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U.\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ C & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485529 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the product ${Q}^{2}\\cdot ({Q}^{2})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{2}\\cdot ({Q}^{2})^{T} & =(Q\\cdot Q)\\cdot (Q\\cdot Q)^{T} \\\\  & =(Q\\cdot Q)\\cdot ({Q}^{T}\\cdot {Q}^{T}) \\\\  & =Q\\cdot (Q\\cdot {Q}^{T})\\cdot {Q}^{T} \\\\  & =Q(I){Q}^{T} \\\\  & =Q{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix:\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n$Q$ is an orthogonal matrix, but it is not a diagonal matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, let $Q=I$ be the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix. Then, \n\n$$\nQ-I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe $2\\times 2$  zero matrix, which is not an invertible matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the product ${Q}^{2}\\cdot ({Q}^{2})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{2}\\cdot ({Q}^{2})^{T} & =(Q\\cdot Q)\\cdot (Q\\cdot Q)^{T} \\\\  & =(Q\\cdot Q)\\cdot ({Q}^{T}\\cdot {Q}^{T}) \\\\  & =Q\\cdot (Q\\cdot {Q}^{T})\\cdot {Q}^{T} \\\\  & =Q(I){Q}^{T} \\\\  & =Q{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix:\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$Q$ is an orthogonal matrix, but it is not a diagonal matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, let $Q=I$ be the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix. Then, \n\n$$\nQ-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe $2\\times 2$  zero matrix, which is not an invertible matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186485535 :question/problem "Find $AB+CB$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 9 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{7} & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -9 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find $AB+CB$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 9 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{7} & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -9 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485535 :question/worked-solution "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB+CB=(A+C)B.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+C & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 9 & 9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -9 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB+CB & =(A+C)B \\\\  & =5{I}_{3}B \\\\  & =5B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}40 & 5 & 5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 15 & 25 & 5 \\\\ -10\\sqrt{7} & 5 & 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB+CB=(A+C)B.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+C & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 9 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -9 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB+CB & =(A+C)B \\\\  & =5{I}_{3}B \\\\  & =5B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}40 & 5 & 5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 15 & 25 & 5 \\\\ -10\\sqrt{7} & 5 & 15\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485538 :question/problem "Calculate $AC+BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n|  |  | $2$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AC+BC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} | $4$ | $2$ |  |\n|  |  | $5$ | {{field-3}} | $3$ |  |" "Calculate $AC+BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | $2$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AC+BC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} | $4$ | $2$ |  |\n|  |  | $5$ | {{field-3}} | $3$ |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186485538 :question/worked-solution "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAC+BC=(A+B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC+BC & =(A+B)C \\\\  & ={I}_{3}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAC+BC=(A+B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC+BC & =(A+B)C \\\\  & ={I}_{3}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485543 :question/problem "Find $AC+BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find $AC+BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485543 :question/worked-solution "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAC+BC=(A+B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC+BC & =(A+B)C \\\\  & =3{I}_{3}C \\\\  & =3C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3\\sqrt{2} & 3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 6 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 6 \\\\ 9 & 6 & 3\\sqrt{5}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAC+BC=(A+B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC+BC & =(A+B)C \\\\  & =3{I}_{3}C \\\\  & =3C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3\\sqrt{2} & 3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 6 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 6 \\\\ 9 & 6 & 3\\sqrt{5}\\text{ }\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485551 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a square matrix\n\nII. $A\\cdot {A}^{T}=I$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a square matrix\n\nII. $A\\cdot {A}^{T}=I$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix"] [:db/cas 17592186485551 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I,$ our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I,$ our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186485577 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a square matrix\n\nII. $A\\cdot {A}^{T}=I$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a square matrix\n\nII. $A\\cdot {A}^{T}=I$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix"] [:db/cas 17592186485577 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I,$ our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I,$ our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186485578 :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186485578 :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore,\n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$ gives a $3\\times 3$ matrix, which would require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$ gives a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which requires finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore,\n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$ gives a $3\\times 3$ matrix, which would require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$ gives a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which requires finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485595 :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following are orthogonal sets?\n\nI. $U=\\{Q([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{Q([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{Q([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])\\}$" "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following are orthogonal sets?\n\nI. $U=\\{Q(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix})\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{Q(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{Q(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})\\}$"] [:db/cas 17592186485595 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nLet's denote the elements of $U,W,$ and $V$ respectively as \n\n$\\,$ $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\},\\,$ $\\{Q({v}_{1}),Q({v}_{2}),Q({v}_{3})\\},\\,$ and $\\,\\{Q({w}_{1}),Q({w}_{2})\\}.$\n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2})=0,\\,Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0,\\,Q({v}_{2})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0.$\n\n- $Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2}) & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=-4+25=21\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the Set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]=0+2\\sqrt{5}-2\\sqrt{5}=0,\\,✓ \\\\ Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=-2+0-2=-4.\\,\\times  \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+0+\\sqrt{5}=\\sqrt{5}\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2}) & ={w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=-10+10=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$W$ only\"." "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nLet's denote the elements of $U,W,$ and $V$ respectively as \n\n$\\,$ $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\},\\,$ $\\{Q({v}_{1}),Q({v}_{2}),Q({v}_{3})\\},\\,$ and $\\,\\{Q({w}_{1}),Q({w}_{2})\\}.$\n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2})=0,\\,Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0,\\,Q({v}_{2})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0.$\n\n- $Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2}) & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=-4+25=21\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the Set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}=0+2\\sqrt{5}-2\\sqrt{5}=0,\\,✓ \\\\ Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=-2+0-2=-4.\\,\\times  \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=0+0+\\sqrt{5}=\\sqrt{5}\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2}) & ={w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=-10+10=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$W$ only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186485763 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -8 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -8 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186485800 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186485801 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186485804 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186485823 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{ali