nd{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(D)={D}^{2}-3D+5I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${D}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =D\\cdot D \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(D):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(D) & ={D}^{2}-3D+5I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4-3+5 & -3+9+0 \\\\ -1+3+0 & 3+0+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}-3X+5 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}-3X+5I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(D)={D}^{2}-3D+5I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${D}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =D\\cdot D \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(D):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(D) & ={D}^{2}-3D+5I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4-3+5 & -3+9+0 \\\\ -1+3+0 & 3+0+5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186486379 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find ${A}^{2}.$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${A}^{2}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find ${A}^{2}.$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${A}^{2}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186486379 :question/worked-solution "To find ${A}^{2},$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find ${A}^{2},$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186486381 :question/problem "If $B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, then ${B}^{2}=$" "If $B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${B}^{2}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186486381 :question/worked-solution "To find ${B}^{2},$ we multiply $B$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -8 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find ${B}^{2},$ we multiply $B$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -8 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186486385 :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ 3xy\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ 3xy\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$"] [:db/cas 17592186486385 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ and $v(x,y)=3xy.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =3y & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & -\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ 3y & 3x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot 3x-(-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}})\\cdot 3y \\\\  & =\\frac{9x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =9u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9u}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ and $v(x,y)=3xy.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =3y & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & -\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ 3y & 3x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot 3x-(-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}})\\cdot 3y \\\\  & =\\frac{9x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =9u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9u}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186486397 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186486397 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186486410 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$"] [:db/cas 17592186486410 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that all linear combinations of \n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nresult in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of the set, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that all linear combinations of \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nresult in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ can't be written as a linear combination of the set, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186486421 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}17 & 15 & -20 \\\\ 10 & -8 & 40 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}22 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -33 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 22\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & 2 & -4 \\\\ b & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}17 & 15 & -20 \\\\ 10 & -8 & 40 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}22 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -33 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 22\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & 2 & -4 \\\\ b & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186486421 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D={P}^{-1}AP$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we find that the eigenvalues of $A$ are\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{3}=22\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-33.$\n\nThe first column of $P$ corresponds to $\\lambda =22.$ Computing $A-\\lambda I,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-22I & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & 15 & -20 \\\\ 10 & -8 & 40 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 2\\end{aligned}]-22[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 & -20 \\\\ 10 & -30 & 40 \\\\ -5 & 15 & -20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 & -20 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -30 & 40 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 15 & -20 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 & -20 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the first column of $P,$ we deduce that ${x}_{3}=0.$ Now, setting ${x}_{2}=1$ and substituting our values for ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ into the general solution, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$" "Notice that $D={P}^{-1}AP$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we find that the eigenvalues of $A$ are\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{3}=22\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-33.$\n\nThe first column of $P$ corresponds to $\\lambda =22.$ Computing $A-\\lambda I,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-22I & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 & 15 & -20 \\\\ 10 & -8 & 40 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-22\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 15 & -20 \\\\ 10 & -30 & 40 \\\\ -5 & 15 & -20\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 15 & -20 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -30 & 40 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 15 & -20 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 15 & -20 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the first column of $P,$ we deduce that ${x}_{3}=0.$ Now, setting ${x}_{2}=1$ and substituting our values for ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ into the general solution, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186486427 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x={c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x={c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186486427 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 10-6\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x={c}_{1}+4{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 10-6\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x={c}_{1}+4{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186486439 :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}],$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$" "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix},$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186486443 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find ${B}^{2}.$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\">${B}^{2}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-5}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Consider the matrix $B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find ${B}^{2}.$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\">${B}^{2}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-5}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186486443 :question/worked-solution "To find ${B}^{2},$ we multiply $B$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find ${B}^{2},$ we multiply $B$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186486444 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186486444 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 5-5\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 5-5\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186486453 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}].$  Find $f(A)$ if $f(X)={X}^{2}+X-1.$" "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$  Find $f(A)$ if $f(X)={X}^{2}+X-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186486453 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+X-1 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+X-I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(A)={A}^{2}+A-I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${A}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{2}+A-I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+3-1 & 0-2+0 \\\\ 0+4+0 & 1-3-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+X-1 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+X-I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(A)={A}^{2}+A-I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${A}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{2}+A-I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+3-1 & 0-2+0 \\\\ 0+4+0 & 1-3-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186487471 :question/problem "How can we best describe the following matrix? \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "How can we best describe the following matrix? \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186487471 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is a matrix for which all entries above and below the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, it is a diagonal matrix." "Notice that $A$ is a matrix for which all entries above and below the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it is a diagonal matrix."] [:db/cas 17592186487475 :question/problem "How can we best describe the following matrix? \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "How can we best describe the following matrix? \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186487475 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $C$ is a matrix for which all entries above and below the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, it is a diagonal matrix." "Notice that $C$ is a matrix for which all entries above and below the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it is a diagonal matrix."] [:db/cas 17592186487576 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}17 & 15 \\\\ -10 & -8\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}17 & 15 \\\\ -10 & -8\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186487598 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186487608 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 24 \\\\ 18 & 40\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 24 \\\\ 18 & 40\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186487643 :answer/value "\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186487647 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186487653 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186487660 :answer/value "\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186487665 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592